<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      INTERNET, for the Google APIs first-run setup calls (FR-101, FR-901, FR-106) and, when
      the write path lands, FR-801.

      Until this line existed the app could not reach the network at all, which was the
      strongest possible form of AC-17 ("no outbound request to any non-Google endpoint").
      That guarantee is now a property of the code, not of the manifest, and it rests on two
      things a reviewer has to check rather than one they could take for granted:

        - Every request this app makes goes through GoogleHttp in :data, which refuses any
          host outside an explicit allowlist and does not follow redirects. It is the single
          place outbound traffic can originate, in the same way SetupEffect.Commit is the
          single place a calendar can be created (FR-105).
        - Play services makes its own calls for the OAuth grant. Those go to Google, so
          AC-17 holds, but they are not ours to route and will appear on a network monitor.

      A networkSecurityConfig would not add anything: it constrains trust anchors and
      cleartext, and cannot deny a host. Cleartext is already off by default at this
      targetSdk.

      Deliberately absent, and to stay absent (§5.2):
        - MediaProjection screen capture (FR-217)
        - AccessibilityService (FR-218)
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      POST_NOTIFICATIONS, for FR-211's capture offer and nothing else.

      **This is the app's first runtime permission**, and it is worth saying what it is not: it
      does not let Latch *read* anything. Reading notifications is the separate, system-granted
      notification-listener access (FR-209), which the user turns on in Android's own settings
      after the disclosure screen. This one is the ordinary permission every app needs in order
      to *post* a notification since Android 13, and without it FR-211's offer would be created
      and silently dropped — which is the failure this line exists to prevent and which lint
      caught before a device could.

      Asked for from the FR-209 disclosure screen, at the moment the user turns the layer on,
      because that is the only feature in this app that posts anything. It is never requested at
      launch: a permission prompt on first run for a feature that is off by default is exactly
      the pattern that teaches people to refuse prompts.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      NOT DECLARED HERE, BUT PRESENT IN THE MERGED MANIFEST. Adding androidx.work for FR-806's
      write queue brings four permissions in from its own manifest, and they are named here
      because this file is the place a reviewer comes to find out what the app can do. Verify
      with:

        ./gradlew :app:processDebugMainManifest
        app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/.../AndroidManifest.xml

        - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE   the connectivity constraint the drain waits on
        - RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED reschedules unfinished work after a restart, which is
                                 literally NFR-302's "device restart while queued"
        - WAKE_LOCK              holds the device awake for the duration of a drain
        - FOREGROUND_SERVICE     WorkManager's expedited path

      None of them widens AC-17: that rests on ALLOWED_HOSTS in GoogleHttp, and every request
      the queue makes goes through it like any other. None of them is a runtime permission, so
      none produces a prompt. FOREGROUND_SERVICE is the one worth watching — NFR-104 forbids a
      persistent background service, and WorkManager satisfies that only so long as work stays
      one-shot and constrained. A periodic worker would be a new NFR-104 decision.
    -->

    <!--
      ML KIT (FR-215) ADDS NO PERMISSION, AND FIVE COMPONENTS. Verified 28 Aug 2026 by merging
      the manifest with and without :ocr and diffing the two — the permission sets are
      byte-identical, so the list above is still the whole list. Reproduce with:

        ./gradlew :app:processDebugMainManifest
        app/build/intermediates/merged_manifest/debug/.../AndroidManifest.xml

      What it does add is recorded here for the same reason the WorkManager permissions are:
      this file is where a reviewer comes to find out what the app can do.

        - MlKitInitProvider              a ContentProvider, so it runs at process start,
                                         BEFORE Application.onCreate. Every launch pays it,
                                         including the text captures that never use OCR.
        - MlKitComponentDiscoveryService a manifest-declared registry of component registrars.
                                         Never started; it exists to be read, not run.
        - datatransport TransportBackendDiscovery,
          JobInfoSchedulerService,
          AlarmManagerSchedulerBroadcastReceiver
                                         ML Kit's logging pipeline. The CctBackendFactory
                                         meta-data names Google's Clearcut transport: this is
                                         what uploads ML Kit's usage telemetry.

      TWO CONSEQUENCES, NEITHER A DEFECT.

      AC-17. This is the concrete form of the restatement recorded in the SRS: a second
      component now makes its own connections outside GoogleHttp's ALLOWED_HOSTS guard. The
      traffic goes to Google, so the criterion holds as written, and it is not ours to route —
      the same standing Play services' OAuth grant has. It WILL appear on a network monitor
      and must not be reported as a failure. Removing these components with tools:node="remove"
      was considered and is not taken: the traffic is inside AC-17, and stripping a library's
      transport is how a crash arrives in a path with no test to catch it.

      NFR-202 holds — ML Kit's telemetry is API usage, latency and error codes, not message or
      image content, and the requirement forbids collecting content. NFR-104 holds too: a
      JobService is scheduled work, the same standing WorkManager already has, not a persistent
      background service.
    -->

    <!--
      THE CAMERA (FR-1201a) ADDS NO PERMISSION, AND THAT IS THE DECISION RATHER THAN LUCK.

      "Capture a business card" on the home screen fires ACTION_IMAGE_CAPTURE, which hands the
      job to whichever camera application the user already has and already trusts. Latch never
      opens the camera itself, so `android.permission.CAMERA` is not declared and must not be —
      declaring it would be a prompt, a Data Safety line and a Play review question bought for
      nothing. CameraX would need it, and a dependency with it; SRS 1.119 records why that is
      reopened only if a device pass shows people retaking photographs.

      No <queries> entry either. Package visibility governs *asking what is installed*, not
      starting an activity, and `startActivityForResult` still resolves the implicit intent — so
      the only thing a <queries> entry would buy is the ability to grey the button out in
      advance, at the cost of declaring an interest in every camera on the device.
      ActivityNotFoundException is caught instead and the screen says what to do.

      The photograph goes to the FileProvider below, in the `camera/` subdirectory declared in
      @xml/file_paths, and the grant is a WRITE, for one launch of the app the user picks. It is
      swept before each photograph and deleted when the capture window closes (FR-1211).
    -->

    <application
        android:name=".LatchApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Capture layers 1, 2 and 4 all land here (§5.2). Each works independently of the
          others, so the activity treats the incoming intent as the only source of truth.

          android:label is "Latch" — 5 characters, within the 6 that FR-202 allows for the
          floating text-selection toolbar.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".capture.CaptureActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/capture_action_label"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Latch.Capture">

            <!-- Layer 1: text selection (FR-201). -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Layer 2: share sheet (FR-205). -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              FR-205's image/* and FR-207's application/pdf. Declared now that FR-215's OCR
              can read them — a share target that accepts a file it cannot read is worse
              than one that does not appear, which is why these waited for :ocr.

              Separate filters rather than two <data> elements in one, because a filter's
              attributes combine: a single filter listing both types would also advertise
              every cross-product the merger derives from them. Two filters say exactly two
              things.

              ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE is deliberately absent. FR-205 asks for a share target for
              text/plain and image/*, singular, and several images shared at once is a
              multi-capture question this app has no answer for yet — each would be its own
              parse, its own chain and its own undo. Adding the filter without that would
              accept the share and silently read one image.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="image/*" />
            </intent-filter>

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Layer 4: Quick Settings tile (FR-213). The supported path for apps where partial
          text selection is unavailable and the user has to use Copy. The clipboard is read
          by the foreground activity, never by the tile — background clipboard access has
          been blocked since Android 10 (FR-214, §8.4).
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".capture.CaptureTileService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@android:drawable/ic_menu_today"
            android:label="@string/capture_action_label"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_QUICK_SETTINGS_TILE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          FR-208 to FR-212: the notification listener.

          The one component NFR-104 permits to be persistent, and that requirement says so by
          name: "No persistent background service on Android other than the optional
          notification listener." It is bound by the system, never started by this app, and only
          after the user has granted notification access in Android's own settings — which they
          reach through the FR-209 disclosure screen and nowhere else.

          `BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE` is a **signature** permission held by the system,
          not one this app requests: declaring it here is how the platform knows only the system
          may bind this service. FR-1104 requires the access to be justified in the Play Console
          as core functionality with in-app prominent disclosure; that disclosure is
          NotificationAccessScreen and the Console half is on FR-1108's release gate.

          Nothing this service reads is written anywhere (FR-210, NFR-206). The message text is
          held in the process's own memory and the notification it posts carries a key —
          see NotificationCaptureHolder for why a PendingIntent extra would not do.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".notifications.LatchNotificationListener"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!--
          FR-1005's `.ics` export, and the only thing this app ever shares out.

          `exported="false"` with `grantUriPermissions`: nothing can read through this provider
          except an app the user has just picked from a share sheet, for one launch, for one
          file. The paths it covers are in res/xml/file_paths.xml and are deliberately one
          subdirectory of the cache — not the database, not the encrypted preferences, not the
          whole of internal storage.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.exports"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>

    </application>

</manifest>
